    {
        "content": "<p>Oh wait, when I put <code>Definition</code> there, it actually turns the <code>Let_In</code> into <code>Let_In {Definition} ...</code> instead of using the constructor?</p>",
        "id": 592115600,
        "sender_full_name": "R Dong",
        "timestamp": 1777569517
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"766585\">R Dong</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/270676-lean4/topic/How.20to.20.60derive.20Repr.60.20for.20mutually.20recursive.20type.3F/near/592115600\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Oh wait, when I put <code>Definition</code> there, it actually turns the <code>Let_In</code> into <code>Let_In {Definition} ...</code> instead of using the constructor?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Using the constructor doesn't make sense here, since <code>Expr.Definition : Operator → Expr → Expr</code>, but <code>List</code> expects a <code>Type</code> (or more generally a <code>Type ?u</code>). Additionally, you cannot use <code>Expr.Definition</code> until <code>Expr</code> has finished being defined, so in particular you cannot use it in the definition of <code>Expr</code> itself.</p>",
        "id": 592119925,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1777570698
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I thought Lean used the constructor as a sort of \"sub-types\" when I saw the inlay hint of vscode showed a <code>{Definition}</code>.  But that was actually implicit.</p>",
        "id": 592125843,
        "sender_full_name": "R Dong",
        "timestamp": 1777572322
    }
